        The difference mean_v5 - exact is sampling noise plus Jensen's inequality (log of a
        small-count mean), which is symmetric-to-negative; the exact term is the systematic part.

PART C  same simulations: truly DE genes reaching Bonferroni p_val_adj < 0.05 (Wilcoxon, log-normalized data):
        sign of reported avg_log2FC vs truth, and error vs the ideal log2 ratio of means
    n1     n2 | ver   n_sig  signflip  medAbsErr   down:med     up:med
    50   4950 | v4       88    0.0000      0.637      0.692      0.640
    50   4950 | v5       89    0.0000      0.010      1.053      1.008
   100   4900 | v4      119    0.0000      0.749      0.627      0.541
   100   4900 | v5      124    0.0000      0.008      1.065      0.969
   300   4700 | v4      200    0.0000      0.520      0.559      0.573
   300   4700 | v5      221    0.0000      0.004      1.129      0.964
  1000   4000 | v4      199    0.0000      0.623      0.381      0.555
  1000   4000 | v5      305    0.0000      0.002      1.085      0.933
  2500   2500 | v4      249    0.0000      0.534      0.577      0.567
  2500   2500 | v5      375    0.0000      0.001      1.056      0.967
        down:med / up:med = median of reported/true log2FC for truly down- / up-regulated
        genes (1.0 = unbiased). v4 compresses both symmetrically (pseudocount 1 on means << 1);
        v5 compresses down-regulated and stretches up-regulated genes when n1 < n2.

